---
import ToolingLayout from "../../layouts/ToolingLayout.astro";
import CopyableCode from "../../components/CopyableCode.astro";
import { InsetText } from "@i-dot-ai-npm/component-library-astro";
---

<ToolingLayout
  title="Set up and use skills"
  currentPath="/tooling/add-and-use-skills"
>
  <h1 class="govuk-heading-xl">Set up and use skills</h1>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    Before you start, make sure you have{" "}
    <a href="/tooling/opencode" class="govuk-link">
      set up OpenCode with AI Gateway
    </a>
    .
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    Skills are reusable prompt instructions that an AI tool can use
    automatically when needed. They help you write shorter prompts because the
    AI only loads the skill when it's relevant.
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    For example, instead of typing a long prompt every time you want to
    summarise meeting notes, you can save the instructions as a skill and just
    type "summarise these minutes".
  </p>

  <p class="govuk-body">You can:</p>

  <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
    <li>
      <a href="#create-project-skill" class="govuk-link">
        set up a project-specific skill
      </a>{" "}
      (so it's only available for a project)
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="#create-global-skill" class="govuk-link">
        set up a global skill
      </a>{" "}
      (so it's available for all instances of OpenCode)
    </li>
  </ul>

  <InsetText>
    OpenCode loads skills automatically based on your request. Use clear names
    and descriptions when you make your skills, so it chooses the right one.
  </InsetText>

  <h2 class="govuk-heading-l" id="create-project-skill">
    Create a project-specific skill
  </h2>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    Project-specific skills are only available when you run OpenCode from that
    project folder.
  </p>

  <ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number">
    <li>
      Open Terminal, type:
      <CopyableCode code="cd " />
    </li>
    <li>
      Drag your project folder into the Terminal window to add the file path,
      and press return.
    </li>
    <li>
      To create a folder for your skill, run:
      <CopyableCode code="mkdir -p .opencode/skills/<skill-name>" />
      where <code class="app-code app-code--variable">
        &lt;skill-name&gt;
      </code>{" "}
      is the name of the skill (use lowercase and hyphens, not spaces).
    </li>
    <li>
      To create your skill file, run:
      <CopyableCode code="code .opencode/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md" />
      where <code class="app-code app-code--variable">
        &lt;skill-name&gt;
      </code>{" "}
      is the name of the skill.
    </li>
    <li>
      Copy and paste the template into the file:
      <CopyableCode
        code={`---
name: <skill-name>
description: <DESCRIPTION>
---

<INSTRUCTIONS>`}
      />
    </li>
    <li>
      Edit the template:
      <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>
          replace{" "}
          <code class="app-code app-code--variable">&lt;skill-name&gt;</code>{" "}
          with a short, lowercase, hyphenated name matching the folder name
        </li>
        <li>
          replace{" "}
          <code class="app-code app-code--variable">&lt;DESCRIPTION&gt;</code>{" "}
          with 1 or 2 sentences covering what the skill does and when to use it,
          for example: "Summarises meeting notes into action points. Use when
          the user pastes notes or a transcript and asks for a summary."
        </li>
        <li>
          replace{" "}
          <code class="app-code app-code--variable">&lt;INSTRUCTIONS&gt;</code>{" "}
          with your prompt instructions
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Save the file.</li>
    <li>
      Check it works:
      <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>open Terminal and navigate to your project folder</li>
        <li>
          run <code class="app-code">opencode</code> to start OpenCode
        </li>
        <li>ask OpenCode to do something that should trigger your skill</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">Example skill</h3>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    This example creates a style review skill saved as{" "}
    <code class="app-code">style-review/SKILL.md</code>:
  </p>

  <CopyableCode
    code={`---
name: style-review
description: Checks pasted content against GOV.UK style and returns a table of issues and suggested changes. Use for a formal style check or 2i review.
---

Output a markdown table with these columns:
- Original content
- Proposed change
- Reason for the change

Only add rows where there's an actual change needed.`}
  />

  <p class="govuk-body">
    This example is illustrative, and the description has been shortened. Find
    examples for:
  </p>

  <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
    <li>
      <a href="https://github.com/i-dot-ai/config" class="govuk-link">
        developer skills and workflows
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/i-dot-ai/skills-and-projects"
        class="govuk-link"
      >
        non-engineer skills and projects
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="govuk-heading-l" id="create-global-skill">
    Create a global skill
  </h2>

  <p class="govuk-body">
    Global skills are available in every OpenCode session. If you have{" "}
    <a href="/tooling/opencode#connect-to-vs-code" class="govuk-link">
      connected OpenCode to VS Code
    </a>
    , you can access your global skills there.
  </p>

  <ol class="govuk-list govuk-list--number">
    <li>
      To create a folder for your skill, in Terminal, run:
      <CopyableCode code="mkdir -p ~/.config/opencode/skills/<skill-name>" />
      where <code class="app-code app-code--variable">
        &lt;skill-name&gt;
      </code>{" "}
      is the name of the skill (use lowercase and hyphens, not spaces).
    </li>
    <li>
      To create your skill file, run:
      <CopyableCode code="code ~/.config/opencode/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md" />
      where <code class="app-code app-code--variable">&lt;skill-name&gt;</code>{" "}
      is the name of the skill.
    </li>
    <li>
      Copy and paste the template into the file:
      <CopyableCode
        code={`---
name: <skill-name>
description: <DESCRIPTION>
---

<INSTRUCTIONS>`}
      />
    </li>
    <li>
      Edit the template:
      <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>
          replace{" "}
          <code class="app-code app-code--variable">&lt;skill-name&gt;</code>{" "}
          with a short, lowercase, hyphenated name matching the folder name
        </li>
        <li>
          replace{" "}
          <code class="app-code app-code--variable">&lt;DESCRIPTION&gt;</code>{" "}
          with 1 or 2 sentences covering what the skill does and when to use it,
          for example: "Summarises meeting notes into action points. Use when
          the user pastes notes or a transcript and asks for a summary."
        </li>
        <li>
          replace{" "}
          <code class="app-code app-code--variable">&lt;INSTRUCTIONS&gt;</code>{" "}
          with your prompt instructions
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>Save the file.</li>
    <li>
      Check it works:
      <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>open Terminal</li>
        <li>
          run <code class="app-code">opencode</code> to start OpenCode
        </li>
        <li>ask OpenCode to do something that should trigger your skill</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>
</ToolingLayout>
